# CHANGE MANAGEMENT 

## Purpose

The purpose of this Change Management Plan is to ensure successful adoption of the Emergency Department Patient Flow Transformation project by preparing healthcare staff, managing resistance, providing appropriate training, and supporting long-term behavioural change. The project introduces new digital processes and AI-assisted bed management capabilities. Therefore, effective change management is essential to ensure the technology delivers the expected benefits.



# CHANGE MANAGEMENT APPROACH

The project will use the ADKAR change management model.

ADKAR focuses on five key stages required for successful individual change:
- Awareness
- Desire
- Knowledge
- Ability
- Reinforcement

This approach ensures that staff are not only informed about the change but are supported throughout the transition.



# ADKAR Implementation Approach

## Awareness

### Objective:
Ensure healthcare staff understand why the change is required.

Current challenges:
- Long Emergency Department waiting times
- Delayed patient transfers
- Manual bed allocation processes
- Communication gaps between teams

Change message:
The transformation aims to improve patient flow, support healthcare professionals, and provide better visibility of hospital capacity.

Activities:
- Staff briefings
- Leadership communications
- Project newsletters
- Department meetings



# DESIRE

### Objective:
Create willingness and support for adopting the new solution.

Potential concerns:
- Fear of technology replacing existing roles
- Increased workload during transition
- Lack of confidence using new systems

Actions:
- Involve staff in solution design
- Listen to concerns
- Explain benefits
- Identify change champions within departments



# KNOWLEDGE

### Objective:
Provide staff with the information and skills required to use the new processes.

Training activities:
- System demonstrations
- User guides
- Training workshops
- Frequently asked questions
- Digital learning resources

Training groups:
- Doctors
- Nurses
- Administrative staff
- Operational managers



# ABILITY

### Objective:
Ensure staff can confidently use the new system in real working environments.

Actions:
- Pilot implementation
- Practical training sessions
- User testing
- On-site support during rollout
- Feedback collection

Support will be provided through:
- Super users
- Helpdesk support
- Change champions



# REINFORCEMENT

### Objective:
Ensure the change becomes part of normal healthcare operations.

Activities:
- Monitor system usage
- Review feedback
- Measure benefits
- Provide refresher training
- Celebrate improvements



# CHANGE IMPACT ASSESSMENT 

The project will impact different areas of the organisation.



## Clinical Staff Impact

Impact:

Healthcare professionals will use improved digital workflows for patient movement and bed availability.

Potential Challenges:
- Learning new processes
- Adjusting to digital decision support

Support:
- Training
- Clinical involvement
- Continuous feedback



## Operational Teams Impact

Impact:
Operational teams will gain better visibility of hospital capacity.

Potential Challenges:
- Changes to existing coordination methods

Support:
- Process training
- Clear role definitions



## IT Teams Impact

Impact:
IT teams will support implementation and ongoing system management.

Potential Challenges:
- Increased technical responsibilities

Support:
- Technical documentation
- Supplier collaboration



# RESISTANCE MANAGEMENT 

Resistance is expected during any major transformation.

Common concerns:

## "The current system works."

Response:
Explain the limitations of current processes and demonstrate how improvements support staff and patients.



## "Technology will make my job harder."

Response:
Provide training, support, and involve users in designing workflows.



## "I do not have time for training."

Response:
Provide flexible learning options and schedule training around operational requirements.



