# TRAINING STRATERGY 

The training approach includes three stages.



# Stage 1: Preparation Training

Before implementation:
- Introduce new processes
- Explain project benefits
- Demonstrate system features



# Stage 2: Implementation Training

During rollout:
- Hands-on training
- User testing
- Support sessions



# Stage 3: Post Implementation Support

After rollout:
- Monitor adoption
- Provide additional support
- Deliver refresher training



# Change Management Risks

## Low User Adoption

Risk:
Staff may continue using existing processes.

Mitigation:
Training, engagement, user involvement, and ongoing support.



## Poor Communication

Risk:
Staff may misunderstand the purpose of the transformation.

Mitigation:
Regular communication and transparent updates.



## Training Gaps

Risk:
Users may lack confidence using the new system.

Mitigation:
Practical training, support materials, and super users.



# ADOPTION MEASUREMENT

Change success will be measured through:
- User training completion rates
- System usage statistics
- Staff feedback
- Patient flow improvements
- Reduction in manual processes



# BENEFITS OF EFFECTIVE CHANGE MANAGEMENT

Successful change management will help achieve:
- Higher user adoption
- Reduced resistance
- Improved staff confidence
- Better project outcomes
- Sustainable improvements



# CONCLUSION
Technology alone does not deliver successful transformation. People and processes determine whether a project creates real value.
Through structured change management, effective communication, training, and stakeholder involvement, the Emergency Department Patient Flow Transformation project can achieve sustainable adoption and deliver meaningful improvements for healthcare staff and patients.
